  "prediction": "**Mountain Walker Dies in Snowdonia Accident**\n\nA 53-year-old woman, Jane Wilson, fell to her death while attempting to navigate a route down from the Tryfan peak in Snowdonia, North Wales. The incident occurred on March 25, when Wilson and her husband, Gary, decided not to reach the summit due to approaching dusk.\n\nUsing only Gary's smartphone to guide them, the couple headed across the mountain's west face, known for its loose rocks and steep inclines. Jane Wilson, an experienced mountain walker and librarian at Manchester University, went ahead to look for a suitable route, but fell 20-30 feet down a vertical cliff and then tumbled about 150 meters down into an area known as Notch arrete.\n\nGary Wilson, who was following behind, heard his wife's cries for help and realized she had fallen. He climbed to a safer ledge and raised the alarm, but unfortunately, his wife's injuries were too severe, and she was pronounced dead at the scene.\n\nThe inquest into Jane Wilson's death heard that the couple had been mountain walking and scrambling for six years and that Jane was a fit and active woman. However, the coroner recorded a conclusion of accidental death, citing the couple's decision to use a smartphone app to navigate the route as a contributing factor.\n\nExperts have warned that relying on electronic navigation aids can be hazardous, especially in areas with limited detail or poor signal. Mountain Rescue England and Wales has urged walkers to use a map and compass in addition to electronic navigation aids, citing the potential difficulties caused by poor detail on electronic maps and the risk of batteries running out.\n\nThe incident has highlighted the importance of proper planning and preparation when engaging in mountain walking and scrambling. The Ogwen Valley Mountain Rescue Team, which responded to the call, has also emphasized the need for walkers to be aware of the risks involved and to take necessary precautions to stay safe.\n\nTryfan, where the incident occurred, is a popular destination for mountain walkers and scramblers, but it is also known for its challenging terrain and steep inclines. The mountain rescue team has reported that Tryfan is responsible for about 30% of the incidents they attend, and experts have warned that the North Ridge, in particular, can be hazardous for inexperienced walkers.\n\nThe incident serves as a reminder of the importance of respecting the natural environment and taking necessary precautions to stay safe when engaging in outdoor activities.",
